# DataEditor — Auto Mode with Existing Markup

## Overview

Auto mode has two faces:

- **Empty root** — DataEditor generates every field.
- **Partially populated root** — DataEditor enhances the fields you've authored, and generates the rest.

The component code is identical in both cases. Only the HTML changes. This guide covers the second case: *auto mode with existing markup*.

The rule is simple: **for each metadata field, if an element with a matching `id` exists in the root, DataEditor enhances it. Otherwise, it generates a field.**

```text
metadata.fields
      │
      ▼
for each field:
      │
      ├── matching #id in DOM?
      │     ├── YES → enhance
      │     └── NO  → generate
      ▼
   rendered
```


## When to use this

Use auto-with-markup when:

- A designer wants specific fields laid out a particular way.
- Most fields can flow automatically, but a few need custom treatment.
- You want to iterate: start with an empty root, author more fields over time, keep everything else generated.
- The same component serves multiple resources, but one field needs consistent positioning across all of them.

If **every** field needs custom markup, use `existing` mode instead. If **no** field needs custom markup, stay with an empty root.


## The component

```semantq
@script

import { DataEditor } from '@semantq/data-editor';
import { Notification } from '@semantq/ql';
import AppConfig from '/public/auth/js/config.js';

const model = 'Product';
const endpoint = '/Product/Products';
const recordId = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('rid');

const excludeFields = [
    'id',
    'createdAt',
    'updatedAt'
];

$onMount(() => {

    if (!recordId) {
        Notification.show({
            type: 'warning',
            message: 'A record ID is required to edit this resource.'
        });
        return;
    }

    const fullEndpoint = endpoint + '/' + recordId;

    new DataEditor({
        root: document.getElementById('data-editor'),
        model: model,
        endpoint: fullEndpoint,
        recordId: recordId,
        baseUrl: AppConfig.BASE_URL,
        layout: 'inline',
        fieldMode: 'auto',
        excludeFields: excludeFields
    }).mount();

})

@end


@style

#data-editor {
    width: 100%;
}

#data-editor .field {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.25rem 0;
}

#data-editor .smq-data-editor-fields {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.25rem;
}

@end


@html

<div id="data-editor">
  <div class="field">
    <label>name</label>
    <span id="name"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="field">
    <label>price</label>
    <span id="price"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="field">
    <label>active</label>
    <span id="active"></span>
  </div>
</div>
```

Three fields are pre-authored: `name`, `price`, `active`. The rest of the metadata fields (after `excludeFields`) are generated.


## How matching works

For each field, DataEditor calls `_findFieldElement(field)`:

```js
_findFieldElement(field) {
  if (!field) return null;
  return this.root.querySelector(`#${CSS.escape(field)}`);
}
```

It looks for an element whose `id` equals the field name, scoped to the root. That's the whole matching rule.

Implications:

- The element must have an `id` matching the field name exactly. `id="product-name"` will **not** match field `name`.
- The element must be inside the root. A matching element elsewhere in the document is ignored.
- Any HTML element type works — `<span>`, `<div>`, `<td>`, whatever fits the layout.
- The element's position in the root determines where that field appears in the final render.


## What happens in each case

| Field state | Result |
|---|---|
| In metadata, matching `#id` in root | **Enhanced** in place |
| In metadata, no matching element | **Generated** into a container |
| In root, no matching metadata field | **Ignored** — left as authored |
| In `excludeFields` | Skipped entirely (neither enhanced nor generated) |

The last row is important: exclude wins over everything. An element in the root with `id="createdAt"` will not be enhanced if `createdAt` is excluded.


## The DOM after mount

Given the component above and a record with fields `id`, `name`, `description`, `sku`, `price`, `stock`, `active`, `category`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`:

**Before mount:**

```html
<div id="data-editor">
  <div class="field"><label>name</label><span id="name"></span></div>
  <div class="field"><label>price</label><span id="price"></span></div>
  <div class="field"><label>active</label><span id="active"></span></div>
</div>
```

**After mount:**

```html
<div id="data-editor">
  <div class="field"><label>name</label><span id="name" class="smq-data-editable" ...></span></div>
  <div class="field"><label>price</label><span id="price" class="smq-data-editable" ...></span></div>
  <div class="field"><label>active</label><span id="active" class="smq-data-editable" ...></span></div>
  <div class="smq-data-editor-fields smq-data-editor-layout-inline" data-layout="inline">
    <div class="smq-data-editor-field" data-field="description">...</div>
    <div class="smq-data-editor-field" data-field="sku">...</div>
    <div class="smq-data-editor-field" data-field="stock">...</div>
    <div class="smq-data-editor-field" data-field="category">...</div>
  </div>
</div>
```

Two facts to note:

1. **The enhanced fields stay in place.** DataEditor does not move them into the generated container. Where you author them is where they render.
2. **The generated container is appended as the last child of the root.** Everything that wasn't matched goes into a new `.smq-data-editor-fields` div, placed after all existing children.

The rendered order is therefore: **authored fields first, then generated fields.** Not metadata order. If you want a specific field to appear between two authored fields, author it too.


## CSS considerations

Because the enhanced fields and the generated container are siblings, styling needs to account for both:

```css
/* Style the wrapper you authored */
#data-editor .field {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.25rem 0;
}

/* Style the container DataEditor generates */
#data-editor .smq-data-editor-fields {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.25rem;
}
```

If you want the generated fields to look identical to the authored ones, you can either:

- Author all fields (and use `existing` mode), or
- Style `.smq-data-editor-field` to match `.field`:

```css
#data-editor .field,
#data-editor .smq-data-editor-field {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.25rem 0;
}
```


## Verification

After mount, run in the console:

**List every rendered field:**

```js
[...document.querySelectorAll('#data-editor [data-field]')]
  .map(el => ({
    field: el.dataset.field,
    id: el.id,
    parent: el.parentElement?.className
  }));
```

Expected: authored fields show `parent: "field"`, generated fields show `parent: "smq-data-editor-fields smq-data-editor-layout-inline"`.

**Confirm the authored fields were enhanced:**

```js
['name', 'price', 'active'].map(f => {
  const el = document.getElementById(f);
  return {
    field: f,
    editable: el?.dataset.editable,
    bound: el?.dataset.smqDataEditorBound,
    text: el?.textContent?.trim()
  };
});
```

Expected: `editable: "true"`, `bound: "true"`, and `text` showing the record's value for that field.

**Confirm the generated fields:**

```js
const container = document.querySelector('#data-editor .smq-data-editor-fields');
[...container.children].map(el => el.dataset.field);
```

Expected: the field names that were not authored, in metadata order.


## Pitfalls

**1. Mismatched `id`.** If the field is named `productName` in metadata but you author `id="product-name"`, no match occurs. The field is generated instead — and your authored element sits empty.

Check the actual field names first:

```js
Object.keys(metadata.fields)
```

**2. Authoring a field that's excluded.** If `createdAt` is in `excludeFields` and you author `<span id="createdAt"></span>`, DataEditor leaves it alone. It stays empty. This is correct behavior — the exclude wins — but it can look like a bug if you forget the field is excluded.

**3. Assuming metadata order.** The generated fields appear in metadata order, but only *after* all authored fields. If you expect `name, description, price` in that order and you've authored `name` and `price`, the render is `name, price, description` — not what metadata order suggests.

**4. Authoring an element for a field that's not in metadata.** No metadata, no enhancement. The element stays as authored. No warning.

**5. Nesting the root incorrectly.** If your authored fields are inside a nested `<div>` within the root, `#name` is still findable via `root.querySelector('#name')`, but the generated container goes to the root, not the nested div. Keep authored fields as direct children, or accept that generated fields will appear at the root level.


